1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,472
Sawasdee. This is Liam, in for Bear, with a short film for INFO seventy-three

2
00:00:04,492 --> 00:00:08,965
seventy-five, Branding and AI. People ask who wins now that AI can code: the

3
00:00:08,984 --> 00:00:12,815
designer, or the engineer. The answer is the one who is both.

4
00:00:12,885 --> 00:00:17,276
Three terms. Agentic AI: AI that carries out multi-step work for you, like Claude

5
00:00:17,296 --> 00:00:21,687
Code building an app. Judgment: deciding what to make, for whom, and whether it's

6
00:00:21,707 --> 00:00:26,414
good. And a creative engineer: someone who can both build a thing and decide it's

7
00:00:26,434 --> 00:00:27,044
worth building.

8
00:00:27,114 --> 00:00:31,899
Start with one experiment. In 2022, researchers from GitHub, Microsoft and MIT asked

9
00:00:31,919 --> 00:00:36,704
professional programmers to build the same small web server. The ones working alone

10
00:00:36,724 --> 00:00:42,249
took about a hundred and sixty minutes. The ones with GitHub Copilot, an AI coding

11
00:00:42,269 --> 00:00:44,836
assistant, took about seventy. Building got cheap.

12
00:00:44,906 --> 00:00:49,106
When building is cheap, the expensive part moves. Anyone can make the box now. The

13
00:00:49,126 --> 00:00:53,888
hard part is deciding what should go in it, who it's for, and whether it's any good.

14
00:00:53,908 --> 00:00:56,420
That's judgment, and no tool decides it for you.

15
00:00:56,490 --> 00:01:01,261
So designers need the tools. With Claude Code and agentic AI, a designer can turn a

16
00:01:01,281 --> 00:01:06,052
sketch into a working thing in an afternoon. Try it, throw it out, and try again.

17
00:01:06,072 --> 00:01:09,945
Producing at that speed used to be the engineer's job. Now it's everyone's.

18
00:01:10,015 --> 00:01:14,361
And engineers need design. For routine code, the tools already do in minutes what

19
00:01:14,381 --> 00:01:19,350
used to take hours, and they keep improving. A good coder who never decides what to

20
00:01:19,370 --> 00:01:22,468
build is competing with the machine, on the machine's ground.

21
00:01:22,538 --> 00:01:26,880
Cheap building changes the loop. You can have an idea, build it, try it, and go

22
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:31,241
again, ten times in a day. The tool will happily make every version. It can't tell

23
00:01:31,261 --> 00:01:33,967
you which one is worth keeping. That choice is yours.

24
00:01:34,037 --> 00:01:37,485
The course names four kinds of work. Ideate: deciding what's worth making. Build:

25
00:01:37,505 --> 00:01:41,752
making it. Brand: making sure the people it's for can find it, and know it's for

26
00:01:41,772 --> 00:01:46,287
them. Ship: putting it in front of real users. Build is the one that got cheap. The

27
00:01:46,307 --> 00:01:48,154
other three are where the value moved.

28
00:01:48,224 --> 00:01:52,474
It changes how people get noticed, too. A working app on GitHub used to take weeks,

29
00:01:52,494 --> 00:01:57,012
so having one said something about you. Now an AI can help anyone make one in an

30
00:01:57,032 --> 00:02:01,282
afternoon, and they all start to look the same. What still sets you apart is what

31
00:02:01,302 --> 00:02:04,218
the tools can't supply: what you chose to make, and why.

32
00:02:04,288 --> 00:02:08,416
That's the creative engineer: a designer who can build, or an engineer who can

33
00:02:08,436 --> 00:02:12,564
design. Either way, what you really bring is your creative sense and your judgment.

34
00:02:12,584 --> 00:02:15,823
When building is this cheap, those are worth more, not less.

35
00:02:15,893 --> 00:02:19,317
Your turn. Pick something you want to build, and paste this into Claude: Here's

36
00:02:19,337 --> 00:02:22,762
something I'm planning to build: one line. Before I build anything, act as a

37
00:02:22,782 --> 00:02:26,206
skeptical design lead. Ask me five questions about who it's for, what problem it

38
00:02:26,226 --> 00:02:30,389
solves for them, and how I'll know it worked. Then tell me which of my answers are

39
00:02:30,409 --> 00:02:34,079
guesses I should test with a real person first. Then check two things yourself. Can

40
00:02:34,099 --> 00:02:38,262
you name one real person it's for? And which of your answers did you have to guess?

41
00:02:38,282 --> 00:02:39,738
Test that one before you build.

42
00:02:39,808 --> 00:02:43,767
The Creative Engineer. At Nik Bear Brown.
